/*
 * Scholarly editorial layer for the ten principal Upanishads exposed on the
 * Scripture Index.  This intentionally overrides the older compact dossiers
 * with source-led prose.  Dates are ranges/relative chronologies, not fictive
 * exact composition years.
 */
(() => {
  const R = window.UPANISHAD_RESEARCH_108 = window.UPANISHAD_RESEARCH_108 || {};
  const OLIVELLE = {title:'Patrick Olivelle — The Early Upanisads: Annotated Text and Translation (Oxford University Press, 1998)',detail:'Critical Sanskrit text, variant readings, translation, notes and an introduction to chronology and literary history.',url:'https://academic.oup.com/book/50014'};
  const UT = {title:'University of Texas Sanskrit — transcriptions of Olivelle’s Early Upanisads',detail:'Searchable Roman-script transcriptions keyed to Olivelle’s edition; useful for checking passages against the edited Sanskrit.',url:'https://sites.utexas.edu/sanskrit/resources/early-upanisads-olivelle-edition/'};
  const COHEN = {title:'Signe Cohen, ed. — The Upanisads: A Complete Guide (Routledge, 2018)',detail:'Scholarly chapters on dating, redaction, transmission, social context and separate studies of the classical Upanishads.',url:'https://www.routledge.com/The-Upanisads-A-Complete-Guide/Cohen/p/book/9780367261993'};
  const BLACK = {title:'Brian Black — The Character of the Self in Ancient India (SUNY Press, 2007)',detail:'Study of dialogue, narrative, social setting and the construction of the self in the early Upanishads.',url:'https://www.jstor.org/stable/jj.18254396'};
  const SHANKARA = {title:'Shankara — Upanishad bhashyas',detail:'Classical Advaita reception.  The commentaries are evidence for medieval interpretation, not evidence that every Upanishad originally taught a later systematic school.'};

  const commonRefs = extra => [OLIVELLE,COHEN,BLACK,UT,SHANKARA,...(extra||[])];
  const set = (name,data) => { R[name] = Object.assign({}, R[name] || {}, data); };

  set('Bṛhadāraṇyaka',{
    muktika:10,date:'One of the two earliest surviving Upanishadic compilations; Olivelle places its principal formation in the seventh–sixth centuries BCE, give or take roughly a century, while stressing that its component materials are not all the same age.',
    setting:'Final books of the Shatapatha Brahmana tradition of the Shukla Yajurveda; transmitted in Madhyandina and Kanva recensions.',
    structure:'Six adhyayas.  The received work is a compilation with major internal blocks rather than a single continuous treatise; the Madhyandina and Kanva recensions preserve important differences.',
    lead:[
      'The Brihadaranyaka Upanishad is not best read as a systematic philosophy textbook.  It is a large late-Vedic compilation in which ritual speculation, debate, myth, teaching scenes and compressed formulas are repeatedly used to ask what remains constant beneath body, status, action and death.  Its famous doctrines acquire their force inside arguments and social encounters, not as detached slogans.',
      'Two features make it central to the history of Indian thought.  First, it contains some of the earliest sustained discussions of atman, brahman, karma, rebirth and liberation.  Second, it preserves competing voices: Yajnavalkya debates priests, answers King Janaka, is challenged by Gargi, and teaches Maitreyi.  The text therefore records a field of intellectual contest rather than one anonymous author delivering one finished system.',
      'Modern philology treats the Upanishad as a layered anthology.  Its Madhyandina and Kanva forms cannot simply be collapsed into a single pristine original; their agreements and divergences are part of the history of transmission.'
    ],
    sections:{
      date:[
        'Olivelle places the Brihadaranyaka, together with the Chandogya, at the beginning of the early Upanishadic corpus and regards both as pre-Buddhist.  That relative position rests on language, prose style, ritual setting and comparison with later Upanishads; it is not an archaeological date.  Precision to a single decade would exceed the evidence.',
        'The work also has an internal chronology.  Some passages remain tightly embedded in Brahmana-style ritual speculation, while others move into extended discussions of self, death, rebirth and knowledge.  Repetition, duplicate teaching scenes and differences between the Madhyandina and Kanva recensions show that compilation and redaction followed the formation of individual units.',
        'A responsible date therefore belongs to the formation of the received Upanishadic collection, not to every sentence in it.  Older ritual formulae can survive inside a later arrangement, and later editorial work can organize inherited materials without creating them from nothing.'
      ],
      structure:[
        'The six adhyayas contain several large literary complexes.  The opening Madhu and sacrificial materials retain the conceptual world of the Shatapatha Brahmana.  The middle books are dominated by Yajnavalkya’s debates and teachings, while the concluding sections assemble genealogical, meditative and ritual materials.  This architecture is more informative than treating all six books as chapters of one argument.',
        'The two principal recensions, Madhyandina and Kanva, differ in order and readings.  Olivelle’s edition provides a concordance between them.  These variants matter philosophically: when a famous formula is interpreted, the wording and immediate literary setting of the relevant recension should be checked rather than quoted from a later harmonized anthology.',
        'The text’s repeated scenes are not merely editorial clutter.  Repetition is one of the ways oral and scholastic traditions preserve, reposition and reinterpret teaching units.  A book-like reading should therefore ask what a passage is doing in its present frame as well as whether a related form occurs elsewhere.'
      ],
      contents:[
        'The Upanishad begins from the late-Vedic conviction that ritual correspondences join person and cosmos, but it repeatedly turns those correspondences inward.  Breath, speech, sight, mind and the sacrificial world are tested as candidates for what is most fundamental.  The inquiry culminates not in a single definition but in several strategies for speaking about the self that cannot be reduced to an ordinary object.',
        'Yajnavalkya is the dominant intellectual figure.  In the Janaka debates, knowledge becomes a form of prestige that can reorder the relation between king and Brahmin.  In the Maitreyi dialogue, the claim that husband, wife, wealth and worlds are dear for the sake of the self redirects ordinary attachment toward an inquiry into the condition that makes experience possible.  In the Gargi debate, cosmological questioning pushes toward the imperishable without converting it into one more physical layer of the universe.',
        'Karma and rebirth appear as part of this larger rethinking of action.  The old sacrificial concern with efficacious acts is not simply discarded; action is moralized and interiorized.  Knowledge, intention and conduct become relevant to post-mortem destiny, while liberation is imagined as freedom from the cycle generated by action and ignorance.'
      ],
      theology:[
        'The vocabulary of atman and brahman is deliberately multivalent.  In different passages brahman can name sacred formulation, cosmic ground or ultimate reality, while atman ranges from living self to the deepest principle of identity.  Later Vedanta schools systematized these terms differently; the Upanishad itself preserves the earlier semantic field from which those systems argued.',
        'The famous negative formula neti neti does not amount to nihilism.  It is a discipline of refusing to identify the self with any finite predicate that can be objectified.  Other passages speak positively through fullness, imperishability, consciousness or the identity of the deepest self with the cosmic ground.  The tension between negative and positive language is part of the text, not a problem to be erased.',
        'Its theology is therefore better described as experimental late-Vedic speculation than as a finished version of Advaita, Vishishtadvaita or Dvaita.  Those later traditions are essential to reception, but reading them back into every layer removes the historical texture that made later debate possible.'
      ],
      critical:[
        'Textual criticism begins with the Madhyandina and Kanva recensions and with the Upanishad’s location in the Shatapatha Brahmana tradition.  Olivelle’s 1998 edition prints an edited Sanskrit text, records variant readings and supplies a concordance of the two recensions.  It should be preferred to quotations copied from unsourced web anthologies when wording matters.',
        'A critical edition does not claim to recover the spoken words of a single historical author.  It establishes a reasoned text from surviving transmission.  For a composite oral-scholastic work, the apparatus and the comparison of parallel passages are as important as the constituted reading.',
        'Traditional commentaries remain indispensable witnesses to reception.  They answer different questions from modern textual criticism: Shankara, for example, explains how the received text functions in Advaita Vedanta, while a philologist asks how particular readings and literary units developed.'
      ],
      reception:[
        'The Brihadaranyaka became one of the foundational texts of Vedanta because it contains unusually dense discussions of self, brahman, knowledge and liberation.  Shankara’s commentary gave many passages a durable Advaita reading, while later Vedantins contested his conclusions by rereading the same scriptural sentences within different theories of self, God and liberation.',
        'Its influence is also literary and social.  Brian Black’s work has emphasized that debates, named characters and teaching relationships are not decorative containers for abstract philosophy: they help construct authority.  Janaka, Gargi, Maitreyi and Yajnavalkya became enduring figures precisely because philosophy here is staged as conversation and contest.',
        'Modern global reception has often reduced the text to a small set of quotable mahavakyas.  A scholarly presentation restores the surrounding prose, speaker, argumentative problem and recension so that the formula does not float free of its literary history.'
      ],
      social:[
        'The text is evidence for elite late-Vedic intellectual culture, not a census of ancient India.  It assumes sacrificial learning, household wealth, kingship and Brahmin lineages, but it also depicts knowledge moving across expected status boundaries: kings can instruct Brahmins, women can participate in philosophical exchange, and debate can determine prestige.',
        'Gargi and Maitreyi should neither be treated as proof of modern social equality nor ignored as anomalies.  Their presence shows that the literary imagination of authoritative knowledge could include learned women, while the broader social world of the text remains hierarchical and patriarchal.',
        'Ritual does not disappear when knowledge becomes central.  The Upanishad repeatedly transforms ritual language into cosmological and psychological interpretation.  The historical transition is therefore better described as reinterpretation and interiorization than as a simple replacement of “ritual” by “philosophy.”'
      ],
      further:['Read Olivelle’s introduction before assigning exact dates to individual passages.  For social and literary analysis, Black’s study is especially useful; Cohen’s guide supplies a recent map of authorship, redaction, transmission and the separate classical Upanishads.']
    },
    references:commonRefs()
  });

  set('Chāndogya',{
    muktika:9,date:'Among the earliest Upanishadic compilations; broadly seventh–sixth centuries BCE for major formation, with older and younger internal materials.',
    setting:'Belongs to the Chandogya Brahmana of the Samaveda and retains a particularly strong relation to chant, ritual sound and liturgical speculation.',
    structure:'Eight prapathakas containing many discrete teachings, dialogues, myths and ritual reinterpretations rather than one continuous philosophical treatise.',
    lead:[
      'The Chandogya Upanishad is a vast anthology of late-Vedic speculation whose subjects range from the sacred syllable Om and the structure of chant to rebirth, ethics, meditation and the identity expressed in the teaching tat tvam asi.  Its size matters: the text cannot be represented accurately by one slogan or one philosophical school.',
      'Its characteristic method is to discover “connections” between ritual, cosmos and person.  A chant can correspond to breath, a bodily process to a cosmic one, or a visible object to an unseen principle.  Later passages increasingly use these correspondences to ask what makes an individual the same through change and what knowledge frees one from repeated death.',
      'The work is also unusually rich in narrative: Satyakama Jabala, Shvetaketu, Uddalaka Aruni, Narada, Sanatkumara, Indra and Prajapati appear in teaching scenes whose literary form is inseparable from the doctrines they communicate.'
    ],
    sections:{
      date:['Olivelle groups the Chandogya with the Brihadaranyaka as the oldest large Upanishadic compilations and places their principal formation before Buddhism, roughly in the seventh–sixth centuries BCE with substantial uncertainty.  The internal materials are not uniform in age.','The text’s close attachment to Samavedic liturgy, archaic prose and Brahmana-style chains of correspondence support its early position.  Yet the anthology also contains developed doctrines of rebirth and liberation; those developments should be studied passage by passage rather than used to force the whole work into a single year.','Redaction is visible in repeated motifs and in the juxtaposition of units with different literary styles.  “Date of composition” therefore means a history of collecting and arranging teachings, not the biography of one author.'],
      structure:['The eight prapathakas are containers for many smaller units.  Early books concentrate heavily on udgitha, Om and chant; later books include famous teaching cycles such as Satyakama’s education, Uddalaka’s instruction to Shvetaketu, the five-fires doctrine, the city of Brahman, and Narada’s progressive ascent under Sanatkumara.','The sequence demonstrates how a Vedic school could preserve ritual exegesis and philosophical innovation in one textual body.  The contrast between sections is evidence of compilation, not a reason to discard the ritual portions as irrelevant prefaces.','When citing the Chandogya, chapter and section numbers should be given because famous sentences derive their meaning from a long pedagogical sequence.'],
      contents:['The opening books reinterpret Samavedic performance.  Sound, breath and syllable become cosmic powers, and mastery of ritual is increasingly expressed as mastery of the correspondences that make ritual effective.  This is the intellectual soil from which later Upanishadic “interiority” grows.','The Shvetaketu cycle in book six begins from the problem of how knowing one underlying reality might make the many intelligible.  Uddalaka uses clay, metal, salt, seed and other examples to lead his son toward the recurring declaration tat tvam asi.  The force of the teaching lies in the sequence of analogies and corrections; extracting the sentence from that pedagogy makes it look more dogmatic and less exploratory than it is.','Elsewhere, the five-fires doctrine links cosmic processes, sacrifice, rain, birth and post-mortem destiny.  The Satyakama story makes truthfulness and education central to the acquisition of Brahmanical knowledge, while the Indra–Prajapati cycle repeatedly rejects inadequate identifications of the self with body, dream-image or sleep.'],
      theology:['The Chandogya does not offer one technical definition of brahman.  It presents a family of claims in which the ground of the cosmos is approached through being, space, breath, mind, name, fullness and the self.  These are experiments in ultimate explanation, not synonyms arranged by a later systematic author.','Tat tvam asi became a major Vedantic proof-text, but its interpretation depends on how “that,” “thou” and the identity relation are understood.  Advaita, Vishishtadvaita and other traditions consequently read the same passage differently.  The historical text should be presented before adjudicating those later debates.','Devotion to a personal supreme deity is less central here than in later theistic Upanishads.  The dominant mode is cosmological and psychological speculation rooted in Vedic ritual language.'],
      critical:['The Chandogya survives within the Samavedic textual tradition, and a critical reading requires attention to pada division, parallel passages and the relation between Upanishad and Brahmana.  Olivelle’s edition supplies an edited Sanskrit text and records significant variants.','Modern interpretation also benefits from literary analysis.  Narrative units should not be flattened into a list of doctrines: the identity and status of the speaker, the student’s error, and the sequence of examples often determine what a teaching means.','Traditional commentaries are evidence for how the text was canonized and philosophically systematized.  They are best placed alongside, rather than substituted for, the philological problem of the early text.'],
      reception:['The Chandogya became a central Vedanta scripture and one of the most frequently cited sources for the relation between self and ultimate reality.  Shankara’s bhashya is especially influential, but competing Vedantic schools developed alternative readings of the same teaching cycles.','Its narratives also entered broader Hindu intellectual culture.  Satyakama became a model for truth and discipleship; Shvetaketu became a type of the learned student who still requires deeper instruction; the five-fires teaching was reused in later discussions of rebirth.','Modern summaries often privilege book six and neglect the extensive ritual-philosophical material.  Restoring the whole anthology reveals continuity between Vedic liturgy and later philosophical inquiry rather than a sudden break between them.'],
      social:['The text’s teaching scenes display a learned world of Brahmins, kings, householders and students.  Some dialogues suggest that specialized knowledge could be associated with royal courts as well as priestly lineages, complicating a simple model in which philosophy moves in only one social direction.','The Satyakama Jabala episode has generated major debate because his uncertain paternal lineage is answered by an appeal to his truthfulness.  The passage is historically important, but it should not be converted into a modern legal rule about caste; it is a literary teaching scene in a specific Vedic context.','Ritual remains socially embedded throughout the work.  The move toward meditation and knowledge often presupposes sacrificial institutions rather than rejecting them outright.'],
      further:['For a first scholarly reading, pair Olivelle’s text and notes with Cohen’s individual chapter on the Chandogya.  Black is valuable when reading the named dialogues as arguments about authority and the self rather than merely as containers for doctrine.']
    },references:commonRefs()
  });

  set('Taittirīya',{
    muktika:7,date:'An early prose Upanishad, probably after Brihadaranyaka and Chandogya; Olivelle places it broadly in the sixth–fifth centuries BCE.',
    setting:'Part of the Taittiriya Aranyaka of the Krishna Yajurveda.',structure:'Three vallis: Shiksha, Brahmananda/Ananda, and Bhrigu; each has a distinct literary and pedagogical profile.',
    lead:['The Taittiriya Upanishad joins Vedic education, ethical instruction and metaphysical reflection in three compact books.  It is famous for the fivefold account of the person—food, breath, mind, understanding and bliss—but that teaching is only one part of a text deeply concerned with how sacred knowledge is learned, embodied and transmitted.','Its opening retains the world of Vedic recitation: phonetics, teacher–student discipline and convocation instructions.  The later vallis move from the human organism toward brahman through nested forms of selfhood and through Bhrigu’s repeated inquiry into the source from which beings arise, live and return.'],
    sections:{date:['Olivelle places the Taittiriya among the early prose Upanishads that follow the Brihadaranyaka and Chandogya and are probably pre-Buddhist.  A broad sixth–fifth century BCE horizon is more defensible than a precise year.','The three vallis need not have been composed simultaneously.  Their different concerns—recitational discipline, the layered person and Bhrigu’s inquiry—suggest the combination of teaching units within the Taittiriya school.'],structure:['The Shiksha Valli is organized around recitation, correlations and student discipline.  The Brahmananda Valli develops the layered account of the person and culminates in the language of bliss.  The Bhrigu Valli recasts the same search as a pedagogical exercise in which Bhrigu successively identifies food, breath, mind, knowledge and bliss as increasingly adequate approaches to brahman.','The five “sheaths” are better understood historically as nested forms or levels of the person in the text’s argument.  The later technical term kosha became standard in Vedantic exposition, but readers should distinguish the Upanishad’s wording from later schematic diagrams.'],contents:['The convocation address places truth, dharma, study, teaching, family obligations and hospitality within the vocation of Vedic learning.  This ethical material is not an appendix to metaphysics; it shows the social form in which liberating knowledge was imagined to be transmitted.','The Brahmananda Valli begins from brahman as truth, knowledge and infinity, then describes the emergence of space, wind, fire, water, earth, plants, food and the human person.  The nested-person sequence connects cosmology with embodiment rather than simply declaring the physical world unreal.','Bhrigu’s inquiry is methodological: his father Varuna does not hand him a final proposition but sends him to practice tapas and return with progressively deeper understanding.  The literary form makes disciplined inquiry itself part of the doctrine.'],theology:['The Taittiriya links brahman with the source and support of beings, with knowledge and infinity, and with ananda.  Later Vedanta interpreted these expressions through competing metaphysical systems; historically, the Upanishad allows several explanatory registers to coexist.','Its treatment of food is especially important because it resists the caricature that Upanishadic spirituality simply despises matter.  Food is a sacred condition of embodied life and an early stage in the inquiry into brahman.','The language of bliss became central to later Vedantic accounts of ultimate reality, but the famous “calculus of bliss” belongs to a graded pedagogical passage and should be read in that context.'],critical:['The text is embedded in the Taittiriya Aranyaka, so its textual setting within Krishna-Yajurvedic transmission matters.  Olivelle’s edition and notes provide a reliable basis for comparing readings and understanding the relation of the three vallis.','Later pedagogical summaries often standardize the five layers into a universal psychology.  A critical reading should first establish what the Sanskrit passage says and only then compare later Vedantic terminology.'],reception:['Shankara’s commentary made the Taittiriya a major source for Advaita discussions of brahman, the self, causality and bliss.  Other Vedanta traditions cite the same passages while rejecting Advaita conclusions.','The convocation verses acquired an independent modern life in educational and ceremonial contexts, while the fivefold person became one of the most recognizable models in popular presentations of Hindu psychology.  Both receptions are historically later than the early Vedic text.'],social:['The Shiksha Valli is unusually valuable for reconstructing the ideals of Vedic education: precision of speech, teacher–student relations, study, teaching, gifts, household continuity and hospitality.  These are prescriptive ideals of a learned milieu rather than a complete picture of ancient society.','The text combines renunciatory-sounding knowledge with household ethical duties.  That combination cautions against narrating early Upanishadic religion as an immediate abandonment of domestic and ritual life.'],further:['Cohen’s guide is useful for separating the three vallis and for the problems of redaction and transmission; Olivelle should be used for the Sanskrit and variants.']},references:commonRefs()
  });

  set('Aitareya',{
    muktika:8,date:'Early prose Upanishad, probably sixth–fifth centuries BCE in Olivelle’s relative chronology.',setting:'Attached to the Aitareya Aranyaka of the Rigveda.',structure:'Three chapters in the commonly studied Upanishad, moving from creation and embodiment to birth/death and the identification of consciousness.',
    lead:['The Aitareya Upanishad is brief but conceptually ambitious.  It narrates creation as a process in which the self projects worlds, guardians, organs and food, then enters the embodied person.  The narrative asks not merely how a universe begins but how consciousness comes to inhabit and recognize a living body.','Its final identification of prajnanam—consciousness or intelligence—with brahman became one of the later Vedantic mahavakyas.  In the Upanishad itself, however, that formula concludes a sequence about life, cognition, birth and the question of who the self really is.'],
    sections:{date:['Olivelle places the Aitareya with the Taittiriya and Kausitaki among the early prose Upanishads following the oldest Brihadaranyaka and Chandogya strata.  The conventional range is broad and relative, not an exact historical date.','The Upanishad’s archaic prose and its embedding in a Rigvedic Aranyaka support an early horizon, while its developed reflection on the self shows the intellectual transformations characteristic of late Vedic thought.'],structure:['The received text’s three chapters form a compact progression: creation of worlds and faculties; human birth and the continuity of life; and a culminating inquiry into the conscious self.','Because the text is short, later summaries can make it seem more systematic than it is.  The mythic creation narrative and the philosophical conclusion are two forms of the same inquiry and should be read together.'],contents:['The opening creation story begins with the self alone and describes the projection of cosmic regions and guardian powers.  The organs of the human person are paired with deities and functions, continuing the Vedic practice of correlating body and cosmos.','Food introduces a practical problem: the newly formed faculties cannot secure it until the embodied organism is coordinated.  The self then considers how it can enter its own creation.  This makes embodiment, not abstract cosmology, the dramatic center of the myth.','The final chapter asks what is the self that one worships and answers through forms of cognition—seeing, hearing, smelling, speaking, tasting, thinking and knowing—before identifying prajnana with brahman.'],theology:['The Aitareya’s ultimate principle is not presented first as a personal creator distinct from the world.  The self is both the starting point of cosmogenesis and the conscious principle recognized in embodied life.','The later mahavakya prajnanam brahma is therefore rooted in a theory of consciousness.  Different Vedantic schools interpret the identity differently, but the early text’s concern is the status of knowing itself.'],critical:['A critical reading locates the Upanishad within the Aitareya Aranyaka and checks the exact boundaries used by different editions.  Olivelle’s edited text is preferable to later anthologies that silently combine or renumber adjacent Aranyaka materials.','The relation between mythic narrative and philosophical claim is an interpretive question, not a textual defect.  Modern readers should resist stripping away the creation story to retain only the final slogan.'],reception:['The identification of consciousness with brahman became a canonical Vedantic statement, especially in later classifications of four mahavakyas.  Shankara’s commentary reads the text within Advaita soteriology.','Modern philosophy-of-mind discussions sometimes recruit the Aitareya as if it offered a contemporary theory of consciousness.  Historical comparison can be fruitful, but the categories of the Upanishad arise from Vedic cosmology and should not be translated directly into modern scientific claims.'],social:['The text’s treatment of birth, the father–son relation and bodily formation assumes a household world and a male lineage of ritual continuity.  These assumptions are historical data about its learned milieu, not timeless prescriptions supplied independently of context.','Its concern with embodiment also shows why “world-denying” is too simple a description of Upanishadic thought: the body is the site in which cosmic powers, food, life and consciousness become intelligible.'],further:['Use Olivelle for text and chronology and Cohen’s dedicated Aitareya chapter for a recent scholarly orientation.']},references:commonRefs()
  });

  set('Kena',{
    muktika:2,date:'An early verse/prose Upanishad later than the oldest prose compilations; Olivelle treats it as probably the oldest of the verse Upanishads.',setting:'Associated with the Samaveda, especially the Talavakara/Jaiminiya tradition.',structure:'Four sections: the first two are largely metrical inquiry into the power behind cognition; the latter two narrate the yaksha episode and its interpretation.',
    lead:['The Kena Upanishad asks a deceptively simple question: by whom are mind, breath, speech, sight and hearing impelled?  Its answer refuses to turn ultimate reality into another object of the senses.  Brahman is approached as that by which knowing occurs, not as one more thing known in the ordinary way.','The second half converts this epistemological puzzle into myth.  The gods mistake victory for their own achievement until an unknown yaksha defeats Agni and Vayu and is finally disclosed to Indra by Uma.  The story dramatizes the limits of divine and human self-knowledge.'],
    sections:{date:['The Kena is later than the oldest prose Upanishads but early within the metrical group.  Olivelle calls it probably the oldest of the verse Upanishads.  This is a relative literary chronology based on language, style and comparison, not a precisely dated composition event.','The contrast between the compressed metrical opening and the prose yaksha narrative may reflect the joining or redaction of distinct teaching forms.  Their conceptual fit is strong, but literary unity need not imply single authorship.'],structure:['The first two sections proceed by paradox: the ear of the ear, mind of the mind and speech behind speech.  The third and fourth sections narrate the gods’ encounter with the yaksha and then identify flashes, thought and disciplined practice as ways of approaching the teaching.','This two-part architecture is central to interpretation.  The prose myth does not merely illustrate a doctrine already complete in the verses; it adds a theology of pride, divine agency and disclosure through Uma.'],contents:['The opening questions shift attention from objects of cognition to the enabling condition of cognition.  A person cannot grasp brahman as one grasps a visible object because every act of grasping already depends on the power being sought.','The famous claim that one who thinks “I know it well” does not know it is an epistemic warning rather than a celebration of ignorance.  The text distinguishes ordinary conceptual possession from a transformed recognition of the ground of knowing.','In the yaksha episode, Agni cannot burn a straw and Vayu cannot move it.  Their cosmic functions prove derivative.  Indra’s approach and Uma’s explanation then turn divine humiliation into knowledge: the victory belongs to brahman, not to autonomous gods.'],theology:['The Kena’s brahman is simultaneously transcendent to ordinary cognition and immanent as the power of cognition.  That double movement explains why both negative and positive language occur in the text.','Uma’s role is theologically significant.  She appears as the revealer who tells Indra the identity of the yaksha, a motif later Shaiva and Shakta traditions could develop, although the early Upanishad should not simply be relabeled as a later sectarian text.'],critical:['The relation of the Kena to the Talavakara/Jaiminiya Samaveda tradition is important for textual history.  Editions also differ in the division and numbering of verses and prose sections.  Olivelle’s text records the critical choices.','The two literary halves should be compared rather than harmonized prematurely.  Their different style is evidence about composition and pedagogy.'],reception:['Shankara’s commentary made the Kena a classic Advaita text on the impossibility of objectifying brahman.  Other Vedantins accepted its authority while interpreting the relation between brahman, the self and divine powers differently.','The yaksha story became particularly important because it gives narrative form to a doctrine that might otherwise remain abstract: even the gods do not possess power independently of the ultimate.'],social:['The Kena is less socially descriptive than the large prose Upanishads.  Its world is one of Vedic gods, teachers and disciplined students; social history must therefore be inferred cautiously.','The concluding emphasis on tapas, self-control and action shows that knowledge is embedded in a disciplined way of life rather than presented as mere intellectual assent.'],further:['Cohen’s Kena chapter is especially useful for the relation between the metrical and prose portions; Olivelle remains the base text.']},references:commonRefs()
  });

  set('Kaṭha',{
    muktika:3,date:'A later metrical Upanishad than the oldest prose texts; commonly placed in the last centuries BCE, with exact chronology disputed.',setting:'Affiliated with the Katha school of the Krishna Yajurveda.',structure:'Two adhyayas, each divided into three vallis; a tightly organized dialogue between Naciketas and Yama.',
    lead:['The Katha Upanishad stages philosophy as a confrontation with death.  The young Naciketas reaches the house of Yama, refuses wealth, long life and pleasure, and asks what happens to a person after death.  The resulting dialogue turns an eschatological question into a disciplined account of choice, self-mastery, knowledge and the deepest self.','Its literary unity and memorable imagery—the chariot, the razor’s edge, the inverted tree, the fire of Naciketas—made it one of the most influential Upanishads in later Hindu thought.  Those images should be read as parts of Yama’s pedagogy, not as isolated motivational quotations.'],
    sections:{date:['The Katha belongs to the metrical phase of the early Upanishads and is later than the Brihadaranyaka, Chandogya and early prose texts.  Scholars disagree over its exact relation to early Buddhism and other late first-millennium BCE traditions.  Olivelle’s broad placement in the last few centuries BCE is safer than claims of a precise century.','Possible conceptual parallels with Buddhist and other shramana materials are evidence for a shared intellectual environment, not automatic proof of one-way borrowing.  Dating arguments must combine language, metre, doctrine and textual relations.'],structure:['The six vallis form a dramatic progression.  The first establishes Naciketas’ three boons and his refusal of temptation; the middle teaching distinguishes the good from the merely pleasant, develops the chariot image and leads toward the self; the later sections elaborate cosmic hierarchy, yoga, the inverted tree and liberation.','The Naciketas fire links older sacrificial achievement with the new quest for knowledge.  The text does not simply replace ritual with introspection; it places ritual within a hierarchy of goals and redirects the final question toward deathlessness.'],contents:['Naciketas’ decisive act is choosing shreyas, the truly beneficial, over preyas, the immediately pleasant.  Yama’s offers of wealth and longevity are therefore not decorative temptations but a test of what kind of good the student seeks.','The chariot image orders senses, mind, intellect and self into a hierarchy of control.  It is not a modern personality chart.  Its purpose is soteriological: an undisciplined person is carried by the senses, while discrimination and control make the journey to the highest state possible.','The text then pushes beyond psychological control toward the self that is unborn and undying.  Death is overcome not by extending biological life but by knowledge of what is not destroyed when embodied conditions change.'],theology:['The Katha combines an interior doctrine of the self with increasingly theistic language.  The highest reality is described through the great self, Purusha and the principle beyond manifest and unmanifest levels.  Later Vedanta schools drew different ontological conclusions from this hierarchy.','Yoga appears as disciplined stilling of the senses and mind.  This is an important prehistory of later classical Yoga terminology, but the Katha should not be read as if it were commenting on the fully systematized Yoga Sutra.'],critical:['The text’s metrical form permits close comparison of repeated verses and parallels with the Bhagavad Gita and other literature.  Parallel wording can indicate shared formulae, borrowing or a common intellectual repertoire; each case requires argument.','Olivelle’s critical text and notes should ground quotations.  Later editions sometimes import familiar readings from commentarial or anthological traditions without signaling variation.'],reception:['The Katha became a favorite Vedantic and modern philosophical text because its narrative makes abstraction memorable.  Shankara reads its hierarchy and unborn self in Advaita terms; modern figures have often emphasized fearlessness, self-control and the razor’s-edge path.','Its relation to the Bhagavad Gita has been especially important.  Shared images and vocabulary show that the two texts belong to overlapping streams of late ancient speculation, but similarity does not by itself establish a simple direction of dependence.'],social:['The story assumes Brahmanical household sacrifice, gift and student discipline, while its central crisis exposes the moral danger of ritual performed without integrity: Naciketas criticizes his father’s worthless gifts.','Renunciatory values—detachment from wealth, mastery of desire, concentration—are incorporated into a dialogue that still speaks the language of Vedic sacrifice and lineage.  The text therefore belongs to a period of negotiation between household and ascetic ideals.'],further:['Cohen’s guide and Olivelle are the safest starting points for chronology and structure.  Comparative claims involving Buddhism or the Bhagavad Gita should be labeled as arguments rather than settled facts.']},references:commonRefs()
  });

  set('Īśāvāsya',{
    muktika:1,date:'A short metrical Upanishad of the later early-Upanishadic period, probably within the last few centuries BCE; its relation to different Shukla-Yajurveda recensions is textually important.',setting:'The fortieth chapter of the Vajasaneyi Samhita of the Shukla Yajurveda; Madhyandina and Kanva recensions differ in verse order and readings.',structure:'Eighteen verses in the common Madhyandina form; the Kanva tradition has notable differences of order.',
    lead:['The Isha Upanishad is only eighteen verses long, but its brevity conceals a deliberately tense composition.  It speaks of inhabiting the world while renouncing possessiveness, of action and knowledge, of becoming and non-becoming, and of a self that is unmoving yet swifter than mind.  Its power lies in holding apparent opposites together rather than resolving them into a simple slogan.','Because the text is the concluding chapter of a Vedic Samhita and survives in differing recensional orders, interpretation is unusually sensitive to textual arrangement.  A reading that ignores the Kanva/Madhyandina evidence can mistake one received sequence for the only possible architecture.'],
    sections:{date:['The Isha is later than the oldest prose Upanishads and belongs to the metrical early corpus.  Broad dating to the last centuries BCE is conventional, but the evidence does not support a precise year.','Its inclusion as Vajasaneyi Samhita 40 gives it a distinctive textual location.  That canonical placement does not prove that the chapter was composed at the same time as the oldest layers of the Samhita; Vedic collections have their own histories of addition and redaction.'],structure:['The common Madhyandina text has eighteen verses, but the Kanva recension orders some verses differently.  The sequence affects how the paired discussions of knowledge/ignorance and becoming/non-becoming are read.','The poem moves through compact paradoxes rather than narrative exposition.  Terms recur with altered force, so translation choices must be justified from context instead of regularized into one later philosophical vocabulary.'],contents:['The opening verse asks the reader to see the whole moving world as enveloped or inhabited by the Lord and therefore to enjoy or protect through renunciation rather than grasping another’s wealth.  The next verse immediately speaks of living a full life through action.  The juxtaposition makes a simple “renounce all action” reading difficult.','The middle verses describe the self as unmoving yet faster than mind, far and near, within and outside all.  Ethical consequence follows from vision: one who sees beings in the self and the self in beings is no longer alienated by hatred or delusion.','The final paired teachings on vidya/avidya and sambhuti/asambhuti are among the most disputed verses in the Upanishadic corpus because the terms can bear several senses.  The text itself insists that paired modes must be known together, and a scholarly translation should signal the ambiguity rather than silently choose a later school’s solution.'],theology:['The word isha in the opening contributed to later theistic readings, but the poem’s theology is not reducible to a developed doctrine of a personal creator.  Lord, self, cosmic order and all-pervasive reality intersect in compressed poetic language.','Its ethical vision is notable: metaphysical insight is tied to how one sees other beings and possessions.  Non-dual-sounding perception therefore has social and moral implications within the poem, not only abstract ontological ones.'],critical:['The main critical issue is recensional.  Madhyandina and Kanva readings and ordering should be checked, especially when an interpretation depends on the progression of paired verses.  Olivelle records these textual problems.','Because the poem is so short, every lexical choice carries unusual weight.  Unsourced English translations often smooth away ambiguity; scholarly presentation should preserve contested terms and explain major options.'],reception:['Shankara’s commentary is among the most influential readings and treats the work within Advaita while negotiating its positive statement about action.  Other Vedanta traditions interpret the opening “Lord” and the paired teachings differently.','Modern religious and political readers have frequently emphasized the first verse as a compact ethic of non-possessiveness and divine immanence.  That reception is important, but it should be distinguished from a philological explanation of all eighteen verses.'],social:['The text contains little narrative social description.  Its most direct ethical language concerns non-grasping, action and a transformed vision of other beings.  Claims about detailed social institutions cannot be responsibly extracted from eighteen compressed verses.','Its coexistence of action and knowledge is historically important because it resists a simplistic story in which Upanishadic wisdom automatically requires immediate abandonment of worldly activity.'],further:['Read the Madhyandina and Kanva ordering side by side in a critical edition before treating the poem as a linear modern essay.']},references:commonRefs()
  });

  set('Muṇḍaka',{
    muktika:5,date:'A relatively late early Upanishad; Olivelle considers it probably post-Buddhist, broadly in the last centuries BCE or around the turn of the era.',setting:'Affiliated with the Atharvaveda.',structure:'Three mundakas, each divided into two sections; predominantly metrical.',
    lead:['The Mundaka Upanishad is one of the clearest ancient statements of a hierarchy between ritual learning and knowledge of the imperishable.  It famously distinguishes “lower” knowledge—including the Vedas and auxiliary sciences—from the higher knowledge by which the imperishable is realized.  The contrast is not anti-intellectual; it asks what kind of knowledge can answer the problem of mortality.','Its imagery is dense and influential: sparks from fire, two birds on one tree, the bow of Om, rivers entering the sea.  These images coordinate cosmology, contemplative practice and liberation in a text that stands closer to the ascetic and renunciatory world than the oldest Brahmana-embedded Upanishads.'],
    sections:{date:['Olivelle regards the Mundaka as relatively late among the early Upanishads and in all probability post-Buddhist.  That judgment depends on language, style and its developed renunciatory/intellectual world, not on a dated manuscript.','Possible Buddhist-era vocabulary and shared ascetic concerns locate it in a wider late ancient debate about ritual, knowledge and liberation.  Parallels should not be converted into simplistic claims that one tradition copied the other without textual evidence.'],structure:['The three mundakas form a clear progression from kinds of knowledge, through cosmology and meditation, to the realized knower.  Each mundaka is divided into two khandas, creating a compact six-part architecture.','The first mundaka’s critique of ritual is carefully targeted.  It does not deny the Veda’s cultural authority; it calls ritual results impermanent and therefore inadequate for one who seeks the imperishable.'],contents:['The text opens with a lineage of brahmavidya and then divides knowledge into lower and higher.  Grammar, ritual and sacred learning can organize the world of signs and action, but the imperishable is not produced as the result of an act.','Sacrificial works are compared to fragile boats when they are treated as final means.  The qualified seeker should approach a teacher with sacrificial fuel in hand—a striking image in which older ritual symbolism is redirected toward instruction.','The two-birds image distinguishes the experiencing agent who eats the fruits of action from the witnessing principle that remains untouched.  The bow image makes Om a contemplative instrument: disciplined attention, not mere repetition of a formula, is the point.'],theology:['Brahman is described as imperishable, pervasive and the source from which beings emerge as sparks from fire.  The text combines transcendence with radical presence: the same reality is the goal of knowledge and the ground of the cosmos.','The two-birds passage became central in Vedantic disputes over whether the individual self and supreme self are ultimately identical or eternally distinct.  The early poem supplies the image; later schools supply rival metaphysical systems.'],critical:['Metrical parallels with other Upanishads and later Sanskrit texts are important for tracing transmission.  A critical edition is needed because familiar verses often circulate independently and can acquire variant readings.','The term “lower knowledge” is frequently mistranslated as if the Vedas were declared false.  Philologically, the contrast is one of soteriological reach: the listed disciplines are valid within their domain but do not by themselves realize the imperishable.'],reception:['The Mundaka became especially important to Vedanta because it supplies explicit categories of higher/lower knowledge, teacher qualification and renunciation.  Shankara’s commentary reads its ritual critique as support for the primacy of knowledge in liberation.','Several verses became pan-Hindu quotations, including satyam eva jayate, later adopted as India’s national motto.  Its modern civic use is a reception history distinct from the verse’s original context within a discourse on truth and the path to brahman.'],social:['The text assumes a world in which Vedic specialists can possess extensive ritual learning yet still be judged incomplete from a liberation-oriented perspective.  This is evidence for contest within Brahmanical intellectual culture rather than an external attack on it.','The ideal student approaches a learned and established teacher after recognizing the limits of action.  Renunciation here is both social reorientation and epistemic discipline.'],further:['Olivelle is especially important for the relative late dating.  Cohen’s guide helps situate the Mundaka within the Atharvavedic and renunciatory developments of the early corpus.']},references:commonRefs()
  });

  set('Praśna',{
    muktika:4,date:'A late prose Upanishad; Olivelle argues it cannot be much older than the beginning of the Common Era, though other scholars have proposed earlier dates.',setting:'Atharvaveda; framed as six students questioning the teacher Pippalada.',structure:'Six prashnas (“questions”), each organized around a named student and a progressively deeper topic.',
    lead:['The Prashna Upanishad is unusually transparent about pedagogy.  Six students approach Pippalada, are required to spend a year in discipline, and then ask six questions about creation, life-breath, states of consciousness, Om and the person with sixteen parts.  The book’s organization is therefore not a miscellaneous list: the questions move from cosmic generation toward the principle that makes the person whole.','Its late position within the early Upanishadic corpus is visible in the way it synthesizes themes already developed elsewhere.  Breath speculation, the paths after death, Om meditation and layered accounts of personhood are reorganized into a schoolroom dialogue.'],
    sections:{date:['Olivelle regards the Prashna as a late prose Upanishad that cannot be much older than the beginning of the Common Era.  Other chronologies place it somewhat earlier, illustrating how uncertain absolute dating remains.','Its synthetic character supports a relatively late position: it presupposes developed Upanishadic discussions of prana, Om, dream/sleep and rebirth and presents them in a deliberate six-question curriculum.'],structure:['The six questioners are named, and each inquiry receives a bounded response.  The order moves from the origin of beings, to the powers sustaining the person, to the source and functions of prana, to waking/dream/sleep, to Om meditation, and finally to the sixteen-part person.','This progression is pedagogical.  The later questions are not random appendices: they move from observable cosmic and bodily processes toward the problem of the self that underlies them.'],contents:['The first question explains creation through paired principles often rendered as life/food or prana/rayi and connects cosmic time with generative processes.  The second asks how many powers sustain a person and makes prana the coordinating principle among the faculties.','The third develops a physiology of prana, its divisions and its relation to post-mortem destiny.  The fourth analyzes waking, dream and deep sleep through the image of faculties withdrawing into mind while a deeper principle remains.','The fifth gives graded results for meditation on one, two or three measures of Om.  The sixth traces sixteen parts back to the person from whom they arise, like rivers losing individual names in the sea.  The closing image converts cosmology into a doctrine of reintegration.'],theology:['The Prashna is less concerned with defining a single metaphysical system than with coordinating life, consciousness and cosmic order.  Prana is exalted without simply being equated with the final self in every passage.','Its Om teaching is explicitly graded.  This is important for later Vedanta and Yoga reception, but the text itself presents different meditative attainments rather than one universal technique detached from Vedic cosmology.'],critical:['Because the work has a clear question structure, textual variants can be evaluated against local argument more readily than in a loose anthology.  Olivelle’s edition should ground quotation and numbering.','The late dating debate should be represented openly.  Claims that the text is certainly pre-Buddhist or certainly from a single century overstate evidence that is mostly linguistic and comparative.'],reception:['The six-question format made the text accessible to later commentators and teachers.  Shankara’s bhashya integrates its prana and Om doctrines into Advaita, while other Vedantins give the person and supreme principle different ontological relations.','Modern yoga literature often extracts the prana passages as physiology.  Historically, they belong to a soteriological cosmology in which breath, senses, mind, action and afterlife are interconnected.'],social:['The opening year of tapas, brahmacharya and faith before questioning is a strong statement about intellectual discipline: access to difficult teaching is conditioned by a way of life.','The named students and teacher frame also show institutionalized transmission.  The text imagines knowledge as something tested through questions rather than delivered as an anonymous revelation without pedagogy.'],further:['Compare Cohen’s Prashna chapter with Olivelle’s late dating and translation notes; do not conceal the scholarly disagreement over chronology.']},references:commonRefs()
  });

  set('Māṇḍūkya',{
    muktika:6,date:'One of the latest principal Upanishads; Olivelle places it near the beginning of the Common Era rather than among the oldest pre-Buddhist prose texts.',setting:'Atharvaveda.',structure:'Twelve compact prose sentences/verses analyzing Om and four “quarters” of the self: waking, dream, deep sleep and the fourth (turiya).',
    lead:['The Mandukya Upanishad is the shortest of the ten principal Upanishads and one of the most conceptually concentrated.  In twelve units it correlates the syllable Om with the whole of time and with four modes of selfhood: waking, dreaming, deep sleep and the “fourth,” which cannot be captured by the predicates used for the first three.','Its later fame is inseparable from Gaudapada’s Mandukya Karika, which turned the brief Upanishad into a major text of early Advaita.  The Upanishad and the Karika must nevertheless be distinguished historically: the latter is a later philosophical work commenting on and expanding the former.'],
    sections:{date:['Olivelle places the Mandukya among the late prose Upanishads and does not regard it as much older than the beginning of the Common Era.  Its highly compressed synthesis and its later association with the Gaudapadiya Karika fit a late position within the principal corpus.','Dating the Upanishad by the Karika would be circular.  The two works have related but separate histories; the Upanishad must already have had authority for Gaudapada to build his argument around it.'],structure:['The text has only twelve units.  It first identifies Om with all this—past, present, future and what lies beyond time—then analyzes the self as four quarters corresponding to waking, dream, deep sleep and turiya.  The final units correlate those quarters with A-U-M and the soundless completion of Om.','The “fourth” is not a fourth ordinary psychological state placed beside three others.  It is described by negation—neither inwardly cognitive nor outwardly cognitive, unseen, ungraspable, without marks—because it is the condition in which the distinctions used to describe the first three no longer apply.'],contents:['Waking consciousness is outwardly cognitive and associated with the gross world; dreaming is inwardly cognitive and associated with subtle experience; deep sleep is undifferentiated in a different sense, with desire and ordinary cognition absent.  The text then refuses to identify liberation simply with sleep.','Turiya is peace, auspiciousness and non-duality, but its description is apophatic.  The point is not unconscious blankness; it is the self that cannot be objectified as one content within waking, dream or sleep.','The Om analysis mirrors this structure.  A, U and M are assigned to the first three quarters through wordplay and analogy, while the soundless/amatrā culmination corresponds to the non-dual fourth.'],theology:['The Mandukya offers one of the earliest compact formulations of non-duality in the principal Upanishads, but later Advaita terminology should not be projected wholesale into its twelve sentences.','Its ultimate concern is the identity of self and brahman articulated through consciousness and sacred sound.  The absence of developed personal-theistic narrative distinguishes it from later sectarian Upanishads.'],critical:['Because the text is so short, its relation to the Gaudapadiya Karika is the chief historical-critical issue in reception.  Citations must distinguish “Mandukya Upanishad” from “Mandukya Karika”; many popular quotations actually come from Gaudapada.','Olivelle provides the edited early text.  Philosophical analysis of ajativada, Buddhist parallels and systematic Advaita belongs primarily to the Karika and later commentary, not automatically to the twelve-unit Upanishad.'],reception:['Gaudapada’s Karika transformed the Mandukya into a major locus of early Advaita and engaged philosophical problems that overlap with Buddhist thought.  Shankara’s commentary on the Upanishad and Karika further canonized that reading.','Because of this reception, later tradition sometimes claims that the Mandukya alone is sufficient for liberation.  That claim is important evidence for the text’s prestige but should not be mistaken for evidence that it was the earliest or historically central Upanishad.'],social:['The Mandukya contains almost no narrative or institutional social data.  It should not be used to make detailed claims about caste, kingship or household life.','Its pedagogical world is contemplative and scholastic: analysis of consciousness and phonology has replaced the extended social dialogues characteristic of the oldest prose Upanishads.'],further:['Read the twelve-unit Upanishad separately before reading Gaudapada.  Cohen’s guide is useful for this distinction; Olivelle provides the critical early text.']},references:commonRefs([{title:'Richard King — Early Advaita Vedanta and Buddhism (SUNY Press, 1995)',detail:'Useful for the later Gaudapadiya context and Buddhist/Advaita debate; it should not be used to collapse the Upanishad into the Karika.'}])
  });

  window.UPANISHAD_PRINCIPAL_SCHOLARLY_V2 = true;
})();